### Describe the bug

When using Solid and testing our app with coverage reporting enabled (powered by istanbul/nyc), there are some branches/functions which are uncovered, but they are not branches which exist in our code, instead they are generated by Solid. Because these code paths are generated, we cannot place a `/* istanbul ignore next */` comment before the appropriate section because it doesn't exist anywhere in our source.

A few cases where this occurs (I'm sure there's others):

* `Show`/`Match` components - If the match is never used, there's an unused function because of the `get children()` method which got generated
* Refs - It generates a ternary with `typeof _ref$ === "function"` as the condiiton. Which in most cases will always be true/false so that means there's a branch of the code which never gets hit.

All of these result in an extra uncovered function here, an extra uncovered branch there. And there's no way we can add the appropriate comments before this code to ignore it.

### Your Example Website or App

https://playground.solidjs.com/anonymous/7b917606-9c75-4695-b669-accb486afc92

### Steps to Reproduce the Bug or Issue

N/A

### Expected behavior

When Solid is generating the runtime code, it should relocate the comments appropriately so they keep the same meaning that they have in the source.

Source:

```jsx
{/* istanbul ignore next */}
<Match when={condition()}>
    <div>Hello</div>
</Match>
```

Generated:

```jsx
_$createComponent(Show, {
  get when() {
    return condition();
  },
  /* istanbul ignore next */
  get children() {
    return _tmpl$();
  }
})
```

-----

Source:

```jsx
let ref;
return <div ref={ref}>Hello</div>
```

Generated: (This should always add the appropriate ignore comments since only one branch will ever be hit.)

```jsx
let ref;
return (() => {
  var _el$ = _tmpl$();
  var _ref$ = ref;
  /* istanbul ignore next */
  typeof _ref$ === "function" ? _$use(_ref$, _el$) : ref = _el$;
  return _el$;
})();
```

### Screenshots or Videos

_No response_

### Platform

- OS: N/A
- Browser: N/A
- Version: 1.8.16


### Additional context

It's also impossible currently to write our own plugin to do this since comments will get totally deleted during compilation when placed in certain spots of the JSX.

For example, this input:

```jsx
function Counter() {
  let ref;
  /* istanbul ignore next -- 1 */
  return (
    <>
      {/* istanbul ignore next -- 2 */}
      <div ref={ref}>Hi</div>
    </>
  );
}
```
Gets transformed into: (the second comment is just totally lost so we cannot even attempt to move it with our own plugin after Solid is done compiling)

```jsx
function Counter() {
  let ref;
  /* istanbul ignore next -- 1 */
  return (() => {
    var _el$ = _tmpl$();
    var _ref$ = ref;
    typeof _ref$ === "function" ? _$use(_ref$, _el$) : ref = _el$;
    return _el$;
  })();
}
```

This is a unique problem with Solid which doesn't exist with many other frameworks because Solid generates a lot of code rather than just calling functions from the Solid library. If these same bits of code were instead unconditional function calls to code in node_modules, this wouldn't be an issue.

**Versions:** solid-js / @solidjs/web 2.0.0-rc.10 and 2.0.0-rc.13; @solidjs/router 2.0.0-next.30 and 2.0.0-next.33. Node 26, server build (`dist/server.js`). Seen in production as a Worker hitting its CPU limit with the document cut off after the shell; `vite dev` does not reproduce it.

## What happens

A `<Loading>` whose children contain a dynamic expression that (1) waits on an async value and then (2) creates a component which creates a memo over an async source and reads it during setup, fails with:

```
<Loading> boundary discovery did not converge after 10001 passes — an async source produces a new pending answer on every retry.
```

This happens even when the source is the router's `query()` or `liveQuery()`, which dedupe the request per render: the fetcher runs once, but every pass gets a new pending answer. Only a source that returns the *same Promise object* for the same question converges.

| Source | Passes | Fetcher calls |
| --- | --- | --- |
| same Promise per question | 2 | 1 |
| `query(fetcher, 'k')` | 10001, throws | 1 |
| `liveQuery(fetcher, 'k')` | 10001, throws | 1 |
| new request per call | 10001, throws | 10000 |

## Why (as far as I can tell)

`processResult` keeps settled answers in `ctx[SLOTS]` by owner id, so a memo created again with the same id reads its value on the next pass. In this shape the id does not stay the same. Logging `getOwner().id` on each pass (rc.13): the component's owner keeps id `101`, but the memo created inside it gets `1010`, `1011`, `1012`, … So the slot recorded on one pass is never found on the next, and every pass starts a new pending answer.

A source that hands back the very same Promise still converges in 2 passes. `query()` returns `cached[1].then(...)` on a cache hit (a new promise each call) and `liveQuery()` returns a new subscriber iterable, so neither can take that route.

## Repro

`probe.mjs`, run with `node probe.mjs query liveQuery` against the published packages (no JSX; hand-written `ssr()` calls):

```js
// probe3's failing shape against the router's real query() and liveQuery().
import { createMemo, Loading, createComponent } from 'solid-js'
import { renderToStream, ssr, escape, RequestContext, createRequestEvent } from '@solidjs/web'
import { query, liveQuery, RouterContext } from '@solidjs/router'

const tick = (v) => new Promise((r) => setTimeout(() => r(v), 1))
let event
globalThis[RequestContext] = { getStore: () => event }
const render = (App) =>
	new Promise((res, rej) => {
		let out = ''
		renderToStream(App, { onError: rej })
			.pipeTo(new WritableStream({ write(c) { out += typeof c === 'string' ? c : new TextDecoder().decode(c) }, close() { res(out) } }))
			.catch(rej)
	})
const errors = []
console.error = (...a) => errors.push(a.map((x) => String(x?.message ?? x)).join(' ').slice(0, 110))
for (const name of process.argv.slice(2)) {
	let calls = 0, fetches = 0
	const fetcher = (id) => (fetches++, tick('v'))
	const source = {
		query: query(fetcher, 'probe'),
		liveQuery: liveQuery(fetcher, 'probeLive'),
		liveIter: liveQuery(async function* (id) { fetches++; yield await tick('v') }, 'probeIter'),
	}[name]
	event = createRequestEvent(new Request('https://app.example/'))
	const Child = () => {
		const value$ = createMemo(() => (calls++, source('a')))
		const now = value$() // setup read
		return ssr(['<p>', '</p>'], escape(now))
	}
	const App = () => {
		const user$ = createMemo(() => tick({ name: 'u' }))
		return createComponent(RouterContext, {
			value: { navigatorFactory: () => () => {}, intent: () => undefined },
			get children() {
				return createComponent(Loading, {
					fallback: 'loading',
					get children() {
						return ssr(['<div>', '</div>'], () => (user$(), createComponent(Child, {})))
					},
				})
			},
		})
	}
	errors.length = 0
	const started = Date.now()
	try {
		const html = await Promise.race([render(App), new Promise((_, r) => setTimeout(() => r(new Error('timeout 60s')), 60000))])
		console.log(name, `calls=${calls} fetches=${fetches}`, (Date.now() - started) + 'ms', /<p>v<\/p>/.test(html) ? 'rendered' : 'NOT-RENDERED', errors[0] ?? '', '| serialized keys:', [...html.matchAll(/probe\w*\[[^\]]*\]/g)].map((m) => m[0]).slice(0, 3).join(','))
	} catch (e) {
		console.log(name, `FAIL calls=${calls} fetches=${fetches}`, (Date.now() - started) + 'ms', String(e.message).slice(0, 140))
	}
}
process.exit(0)
```

## Expected

The boundary settles once the answer has arrived: either the memo gets the same owner id on every pass, so its settled slot is found, or `query()` / `liveQuery()` hand back an answer the server recognises as settled on the next pass.

## Platform

- OS: macOS 26 (darwin arm64)
- Node: 26.10.0
- solid-js / @solidjs/web: 2.0.0-rc.10, 2.0.0-rc.13
- @solidjs/router: 2.0.0-next.30, 2.0.0-next.33

A memo created with the (internal) `{ transparent: true }` option inside a component while `sharedConfig.hydrating` is true causes every element AFTER the creating component to fail its claim:

```
Hydration tag mismatch for key "2": expected <pre> but found [object HTMLElement]
[REACTIVITY_HALTED] ... TypeError: Cannot read properties of null (reading 'nextSibling')
Hydration completed with 1 unclaimed server-rendered node(s): <pre _hk="3" ...>
```

The unclaimed nodes stay visible but no client scope owns them — bindings in them are permanently dead. In an app the failure is silent-but-fatal: a footer rendered after such a component keeps its SSR appearance and never reacts to state.

## Minimal reproduction

vite 8.2.1 + vite-plugin-solid 3.0.0-next.27, solid-js / @solidjs/web 2.0.0-rc.0, `renderToStream` on the server, `hydrate` on the client. `src/App.tsx` is the whole story:

```tsx
import { createMemo, createSignal } from 'solid-js';

function Inner(props: { disabled: boolean }) {
	const pending = createMemo(() => false, { transparent: true });
	return (
		<nav>
			<button id="continue" disabled={props.disabled || pending()}>
				Continue
			</button>
		</nav>
	);
}

export default function App() {
	const [checked, setChecked] = createSignal(false);
	return (
		<>
			<label>
				<input
					type="checkbox"
					checked={checked()}
					onChange={(e) => setChecked(e.currentTarget.checked)}
				/>{' '}
				done
			</label>
			<Inner disabled={!checked()} />
			<pre id="after">sibling after Inner: {String(checked())}</pre>
		</>
	);
}
```

Observed:

- With the transparent memo: the `<pre>` fails to claim, hydration reports it unclaimed, the REACTIVITY_HALTED TypeError fires, and the checkbox no longer enables the button.
- Remove `{ transparent: true }` (or the memo entirely): hydration is clean and everything works.
- The memo's value never changes and is even never read in the broken variant — creating it is enough.

